JRBrown wrote:
> Clueless newbie here. I am playing Nethack v3.4.3 (official binaries)
> on a Mac running OS 8.6 (ancient, I know...).
This is the first time that I have known of anyone running NetHack over
classic Mac OS! I have never tried it myself; any Mac that I find has
Mac OS X. You must be using the Carbon port.

> My problem: I can't figure out how to restore a saved game.  When I
> save a game I get a file named something like "save/1Name"...
For Unix (including Mac OS X) the / is the file separator, meaning that
"save/1Name" refers to a file called "1Name" inside of a folder called
"save". For classic Mac OS like you have, the : is the file separator.
If you are having a file called "save/1Name", then NetHack (or
CarbonLib) might be confusing "save/1Name" with "save:1Name". It is the
difference between a wand and a lizard.

> ... but the save-file still disappears.
The save-file should never disappear unless NetHack actually runs your
saved game. It is probably a bug. The bug might be different than my
guess above.

-----

CarbonLib (Classic Mac OS) and Carbon.framework (Mac OS X) implements
the compatibility that allows Carbon programs like NetHack to run on
both systems. So first I suggest that you try updating your CarbonLib,
to know whether it fixes the problem. I would tell you to open the
Software Update control panel, but Apple did not add that until OS 9.

However I found this:
http://docs.info.apple.com/article.html?artnum=120017

Try downloading and installing this CarbonLib 1.3.1 onto your computer.
(If I recall well, then CarbonLib is a file in the Extensions folder in
the System Folder, in case you want to check your current version.)

If that does not work, then my best guess is that NetHack does not work
with OS 8.6, in which case I am not surprised that no one noticed this
before. Then report the bug to the DevTeam (I do not see the bug at
http://www.nethack.org/v343/bugs.html#M).

You could still telnet to a public server. (This will give you the
ASCII interface to NetHack, not the tiles interface, and will force you
to use the keyboard yuhjklbn to move your character.) I no longer
install (vanilla 3.4.3) NetHack myself because of
http://alt.org/nethack/ - If you need a telnet client then you might
investigate BetterTelnet http://www.club.cc.cmu.edu/~rbraun/macssh/ - I
never used it but I have used MacSSH which does SSH not telnet.

